<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Sayn-Wittgenstein-Berleburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1605 CE – 1806 CE · capital Bad Berleburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Westphalian county in the Rothaar hills, known as a refuge for radical Pietists and as home of the Berleburg Bible.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-sayn">County of Sayn</a>-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-wittgenstein">Wittgenstein</a> was divided in 1605 among the sons of Count Ludwig I. Georg received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-berleburg">Berleburg</a> in the northern Wittgenstein land, founding a line that would persist alongside Sayn-Wittgenstein-Hohenstein.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The county was poor, forested upland with some iron working, governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berleburg-castle">Berleburg Castle</a>. Under Count Casimir in the early 18th century it became a haven for religious dissenters and radical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietism">Pietists</a>, and the eight-volume <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berleburg-bible">Berleburg Bible</a>, a mystical commentary, was published there from 1726. The counts were raised to princely rank in 1792.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The territory was mediatised in 1806 and placed under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse</a>, then passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a> in 1816 as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-westphalia">Province of Westphalia</a>. The princes of Sayn-Wittgenstein-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-berleburg">Berleburg</a> still live at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berleburg-castle">Berleburg Castle</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-sayn-wittgenstein-berleburg.jpg" alt="County of Sayn-Wittgenstein-Berleburg" width="320" height="341"><figcaption>Image: Felipe Fidelis Tobias This W3C-unspecified vector image was created with Inkscape . · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_County_of_Sayn-Wittgenstein-Berleburg_(1607%E2%80%931806).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1605 CE – 1806 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bad Berleburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Georg, Casimir</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sayn-Wittgenstein-Berleburg, Wittgenstein</dd></dl></aside></div>
